1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,280
Hello everyone, and welcome to Two Cups English.

2
00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:06,699
I'm Mia, and Ben is here with me.

3
00:00:06,780 --> 00:00:09,540
This podcast is for people who are learning English.

4
00:00:10,220 --> 00:00:11,060
We speak slowly.

5
00:00:11,620 --> 00:00:12,980
We use simple words.

6
00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:16,419
And we repeat the important words many times.

7
00:00:17,360 --> 00:00:19,360
Today, our topic is hello.

8
00:00:19,910 --> 00:00:20,940
The first hello.

9
00:00:21,940 --> 00:00:23,180
Saying hello first.

10
00:00:24,280 --> 00:00:26,360
Ben, who did you say hello to today?

11
00:00:27,640 --> 00:00:28,400
Hi Mia.

12
00:00:28,460 --> 00:00:29,060
Hi everyone.

13
00:00:29,700 --> 00:00:32,860
Today, I said hello to the bus driver, and to you.

14
00:00:33,700 --> 00:00:34,120
That is all.

15
00:00:35,880 --> 00:00:38,560
And there is a man at the corner shop.

16
00:00:39,440 --> 00:00:42,000
I have bought bread from him for four years.

17
00:00:42,880 --> 00:00:43,840
Four years.

18
00:00:44,500 --> 00:00:46,420
And I have never asked his name.

19
00:00:47,480 --> 00:00:48,260
What about you, Mia?

20
00:00:48,920 --> 00:00:49,720
Any hellos today?

21
00:00:50,820 --> 00:00:52,000
One big one, Ben.

22
00:00:52,760 --> 00:00:54,700
There is a woman who lives next door to me.

23
00:00:55,200 --> 00:00:58,240
For two years, we met in the hallway almost every day.

24
00:00:58,900 --> 00:01:01,220
And for two years, we said nothing.

25
00:01:01,800 --> 00:01:02,580
A small nod.

26
00:01:02,960 --> 00:01:03,640
Eyes down.

27
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:04,640
Keys out.

28
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:05,820
Door closed.

29
00:01:06,360 --> 00:01:07,400
Two years.

30
00:01:08,440 --> 00:01:10,080
Two years of nodding, Mia.

31
00:01:10,880 --> 00:01:14,660
Two years is a long time to stand in a hallway and say nothing.

32
00:01:15,650 --> 00:01:16,110
And today?

33
00:01:16,900 --> 00:01:17,790
Did something change?

34
00:01:19,140 --> 00:01:20,380
Last week, actually, Ben.

35
00:01:20,980 --> 00:01:24,980
I was tired, and I did not think, and I said, "Hello.

36
00:01:25,440 --> 00:01:27,520
Long day?" And she stopped.

37
00:01:28,020 --> 00:01:29,179
And she smiled.

38
00:01:29,200 --> 00:01:34,300
And she said, "The longest." And we talked for five minutes about nothing.

39
00:01:34,840 --> 00:01:36,920
And now, every day, we say hello.

40
00:01:37,540 --> 00:01:38,580
We are almost friends.

41
00:01:39,200 --> 00:01:41,850
Two years of silence, and one word fixed it.

42
00:01:42,930 --> 00:01:48,920
"Two years of silence, and one word fixed it." Mia, that is our topic today.

43
00:01:49,960 --> 00:01:51,100
So here is our plan.

44
00:01:52,040 --> 00:01:55,460
In part one, we talk about our own silent people.

45
00:01:56,260 --> 00:01:57,890
The neighbor, the bread man.

46
00:01:59,040 --> 00:02:02,620
In part two, we ask why hello matters so much.

47
00:02:03,460 --> 00:02:05,900
And I have a true story for that.

48
00:02:05,960 --> 00:02:09,280
The longest study in the world.

49
00:02:09,340 --> 00:02:13,180
In part three, we share simple ways to say hello first.

50
00:02:14,060 --> 00:02:14,840
Does that sound good?

51
00:02:16,140 --> 00:02:17,100
That sounds good, Ben.

52
00:02:17,720 --> 00:02:21,360
And to everyone listening, please do not worry if you miss something.

53
00:02:21,800 --> 00:02:23,280
You will hear it again.

54
00:02:23,320 --> 00:02:29,200
You can listen while you walk, while you cook, or on the bus, next to a person you have never talked to.

55
00:02:30,140 --> 00:02:30,660
Okay, Ben.

56
00:02:30,960 --> 00:02:31,440
Part one.

57
00:02:32,220 --> 00:02:33,920
Why did it take me two years?

58
00:02:34,460 --> 00:02:36,180
What do you think?

59
00:02:36,400 --> 00:02:38,680
I think hello is small, Mia.

60
00:02:38,690 --> 00:02:40,520
And small things feel like they can wait.

61
00:02:41,400 --> 00:02:43,680
So people wait, and nobody talks.

62
00:02:44,710 --> 00:02:54,760
"I will say hello tomorrow." "Next time." "When it feels natural." And it never feels natural, because the silence gets heavier every day.

63
00:02:55,880 --> 00:02:57,240
Day one, hello is easy.

64
00:02:58,220 --> 00:03:00,480
Day one hundred, hello is strange.

65
00:03:01,560 --> 00:03:05,060
Day seven hundred, hello feels almost impossible.

66
00:03:06,080 --> 00:03:07,560
"The silence was not empty.

67
00:03:07,940 --> 00:03:10,140
It was growing." Yes, Ben.

68
00:03:10,520 --> 00:03:13,410
And I was feeding it every morning, with my eyes down.

69
00:03:14,160 --> 00:03:14,940
And your bread man?

70
00:03:15,720 --> 00:03:16,560
Four years.

71
00:03:17,200 --> 00:03:18,280
Why not ask his name?

72
00:03:19,680 --> 00:03:23,400
Because after four years, asking feels strange, Mia.

73
00:03:24,510 --> 00:03:27,690
"Sorry, what is your name?" After four years?

74
00:03:28,760 --> 00:03:29,980
So I do not ask.

75
00:03:30,680 --> 00:03:32,380
And the silence gets another day.

76
00:03:33,360 --> 00:03:35,120
Four years and one day.

77
00:03:37,320 --> 00:03:39,120
Four years and one day.

78
00:03:40,080 --> 00:03:42,440
Ben, you are going to ask him tomorrow.

79
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:44,920
I am, Mia.

80
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:46,860
I promise, in front of everyone.

81
00:03:47,480 --> 00:03:49,860
Tomorrow, the bread man gets a name.

82
00:03:50,800 --> 00:03:52,680
And I know a trick for the late name, actually.

83
00:03:53,100 --> 00:03:53,620
You taught me.

84
00:03:54,040 --> 00:03:54,620
I did, Ben.

85
00:03:55,400 --> 00:03:55,960
Say this.

86
00:03:56,860 --> 00:03:57,920
"I am terrible.

87
00:03:58,660 --> 00:04:02,200
I have been coming here for years, and I never asked your name.

88
00:04:03,160 --> 00:04:05,890
I am Ben." And people love that.

89
00:04:06,490 --> 00:04:08,120
They do not think you are strange.

90
00:04:08,660 --> 00:04:10,439
They think you are honest.

91
00:04:10,460 --> 00:04:13,460
And they say their name, and now you are two people.

92
00:04:14,020 --> 00:04:15,520
Not a customer and a shop.

93
00:04:16,120 --> 00:04:17,280
Maybe friends, one day.

94
00:04:17,780 --> 00:04:23,560
"Two people, not a customer and a shop." I am going to use exactly that sentence, Mia.

95
00:04:24,660 --> 00:04:26,640
Can I tell you about a party last winter?

96
00:04:27,160 --> 00:04:29,160
Because it taught me something about "first."

97
00:04:29,700 --> 00:04:30,480
A party, Ben?

98
00:04:31,300 --> 00:04:32,510
You do not like parties.

99
00:04:33,090 --> 00:04:35,200
You told me you hide in the kitchen at parties.

100
00:04:36,500 --> 00:04:37,020
I do, Mia.

101
00:04:37,780 --> 00:04:39,080
Twenty people.

102
00:04:39,160 --> 00:04:40,880
Everyone looking at phones.

103
00:04:40,940 --> 00:04:41,760
Nobody talking.

104
00:04:42,620 --> 00:04:45,500
And I remembered a rule my grandfather told me when I was a boy.

105
00:04:46,450 --> 00:04:49,440
"Ben, in a room, be the first to say hello.

106
00:04:50,140 --> 00:04:51,170
Everyone is waiting.

107
00:04:51,800 --> 00:04:53,600
Be the one who stops the waiting."

108
00:04:54,020 --> 00:04:55,080
"Everyone is waiting.

109
00:04:55,720 --> 00:04:59,800
Be the one who stops the waiting." Ben, that is beautiful.

110
00:05:00,700 --> 00:05:01,940
So what did you do at the party?

111
00:05:02,420 --> 00:05:04,400
I said hello to the man next to me, Mia.

112
00:05:05,100 --> 00:05:05,480
First.

113
00:05:06,300 --> 00:05:08,440
And he almost fell off his chair with relief.

114
00:05:09,080 --> 00:05:10,520
He had been waiting for an hour.

115
00:05:11,200 --> 00:05:12,430
We talked all evening.

116
00:05:13,400 --> 00:05:14,310
Everyone is waiting.

117
00:05:14,900 --> 00:05:16,900
Be the one who goes first.

118
00:05:16,910 --> 00:05:18,680
And that makes the fear smaller, Ben.

119
00:05:19,440 --> 00:05:23,080
Because if everyone is waiting, then my hello is not strange.

120
00:05:23,740 --> 00:05:25,360
It is the thing everyone wants.

121
00:05:26,240 --> 00:05:28,700
I am not the strange person who talks.

122
00:05:28,840 --> 00:05:31,140
I am the person who ends the waiting.

123
00:05:31,170 --> 00:05:34,740
The first hello is a gift to the whole room, not a risk for me.

124
00:05:35,300 --> 00:05:36,860
A gift, not a risk.

125
00:05:37,340 --> 00:05:37,860
Yes, Mia.

126
00:05:38,660 --> 00:05:41,500
But let me ask about the fear, because it is real.

127
00:05:42,480 --> 00:05:43,840
Why is hello hard for you?

128
00:05:44,680 --> 00:05:45,480
What is the fear?

129
00:05:46,500 --> 00:05:47,480
Small fears, Ben.

130
00:05:48,200 --> 00:05:49,400
What if she does not answer?

131
00:05:50,300 --> 00:05:51,820
What if she thinks I am strange?

132
00:05:52,600 --> 00:05:54,100
What if she does not want a friend?

133
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:57,660
What if she wants to talk for an hour and I am tired?

134
00:05:58,700 --> 00:06:00,740
Small fears, but they are enough.

135
00:06:01,620 --> 00:06:05,560
Because hello is small, and a small fear can stop a small thing.

136
00:06:06,340 --> 00:06:08,340
A big fear cannot stop a big thing.

137
00:06:09,140 --> 00:06:13,500
But a small fear can stop hello, every day, for two years.

138
00:06:13,870 --> 00:06:18,270
"A small fear can stop a small thing." Mia, that is true.

139
00:06:19,140 --> 00:06:20,560
And I think there is another reason.

140
00:06:21,280 --> 00:06:22,680
We wait for the other person.

141
00:06:23,400 --> 00:06:26,240
I waited four years for the bread man to ask my name.

142
00:06:27,020 --> 00:06:28,800
Maybe he waited four years for me.

143
00:06:29,780 --> 00:06:33,749
Two people, both waiting, both polite, both silent.

144
00:06:34,500 --> 00:06:37,020
Both waiting, both silent.

145
00:06:38,360 --> 00:06:42,060
So the word "first" in today's title is the important word, Ben.

146
00:06:42,760 --> 00:06:44,340
Say hello first.

147
00:06:44,800 --> 00:06:48,020
Not "say hello when they do." First.

148
00:06:48,700 --> 00:06:51,680
Somebody has to go first, and it might as well be you.

149
00:06:53,260 --> 00:06:54,280
It should be you, Mia.

150
00:06:54,640 --> 00:06:56,860
Because then it happens today.

151
00:06:56,870 --> 00:06:59,190
And tell me, what happened after the hallway hello?

152
00:06:59,660 --> 00:07:01,180
Did it stop there?

153
00:07:01,260 --> 00:07:03,020
It did not stop, Ben.

154
00:07:03,060 --> 00:07:05,260
The next day, she said hello first.

155
00:07:05,660 --> 00:07:08,140
And the day after, she talked about my plants.

156
00:07:08,620 --> 00:07:11,100
And last Sunday, she brought me a piece of cake.

157
00:07:11,600 --> 00:07:16,280
A whole piece of cake, from a person who was a closed door for two years.

158
00:07:16,760 --> 00:07:18,020
Now she is a friend.

159
00:07:18,700 --> 00:07:20,820
All of that was inside one hello.

160
00:07:22,160 --> 00:07:22,780
Cake, Mia.

161
00:07:23,200 --> 00:07:25,520
Behind the door was cake and a friend.

162
00:07:25,950 --> 00:07:27,500
That is what first hellos do.

163
00:07:28,040 --> 00:07:30,000
They open something you cannot see yet.

164
00:07:31,100 --> 00:07:32,560
Okay, so that was part one.

165
00:07:33,120 --> 00:07:33,989
Our silent people.

166
00:07:34,510 --> 00:07:38,530
A neighbor, a bread man, a party, and a piece of cake.

167
00:07:39,400 --> 00:07:41,220
Now, part two.

168
00:07:41,300 --> 00:07:42,940
Why does hello matter so much?

169
00:07:44,060 --> 00:07:46,500
Mia, this is the true story I promised.

170
00:07:46,560 --> 00:07:48,320
The longest study in the world.

171
00:07:49,160 --> 00:07:51,739
The longest study in the world, Ben?

172
00:07:51,820 --> 00:07:52,990
How long is long?

173
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:55,060
Eighty years, Mia.

174
00:07:56,080 --> 00:07:57,460
Okay, part two.

175
00:07:57,520 --> 00:07:58,800
This is a true story.

176
00:07:59,780 --> 00:08:06,040
In 1938, in America, scientists at Harvard University started to follow a group of young men.

177
00:08:06,690 --> 00:08:07,980
They wanted to know one thing.

178
00:08:08,760 --> 00:08:09,950
What makes a good life?

179
00:08:10,700 --> 00:08:13,020
And they followed the same people for their whole lives.

180
00:08:13,620 --> 00:08:15,300
Every two years, questions.

181
00:08:15,880 --> 00:08:17,700
Every five years, health checks.

182
00:08:18,300 --> 00:08:20,220
They are still following the families today.

183
00:08:20,910 --> 00:08:25,940
The man who leads the study now, Robert Waldinger, gave a talk about it in 2015.

184
00:08:26,960 --> 00:08:28,660
Eighty years of questions, Ben.

185
00:08:29,140 --> 00:08:30,000
What did they find?

186
00:08:30,460 --> 00:08:30,760
Money?

187
00:08:31,260 --> 00:08:31,620
Work?

188
00:08:32,060 --> 00:08:32,480
Fame?

189
00:08:33,700 --> 00:08:34,480
Not money, Mia.

190
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:36,040
Not fame.

191
00:08:36,100 --> 00:08:36,890
Not hard work.

192
00:08:37,580 --> 00:08:40,370
The clearest message from eighty years of data was this.

193
00:08:41,080 --> 00:08:43,760
Good relationships keep us happier and healthier.

194
00:08:44,520 --> 00:08:45,780
Not many relationships.

195
00:08:46,180 --> 00:08:46,680
Good ones.

196
00:08:47,440 --> 00:08:48,140
Warm ones.

197
00:08:48,820 --> 00:08:52,280
People with warm connections at fifty were the healthiest at eighty.

198
00:08:53,070 --> 00:08:56,150
And lonely people were less healthy, and they died earlier.

199
00:08:57,130 --> 00:09:00,560
Loneliness, he said, was as bad for the body as smoking.

200
00:09:01,970 --> 00:09:05,680
"As bad as smoking." Ben, that is a strong sentence.

201
00:09:06,200 --> 00:09:09,560
So the hello in the hallway is not a small polite thing.

202
00:09:09,620 --> 00:09:10,290
It is health.

203
00:09:10,780 --> 00:09:12,610
It is the thing the study was measuring.

204
00:09:13,480 --> 00:09:14,880
Exactly, Mia.

205
00:09:14,940 --> 00:09:16,420
We think of hello as manners.

206
00:09:16,820 --> 00:09:17,520
Something nice.

207
00:09:18,060 --> 00:09:18,480
Optional.

208
00:09:19,140 --> 00:09:22,740
But eighty years of science says that warm connection is the main thing.

209
00:09:23,320 --> 00:09:24,420
Not one of the things.

210
00:09:24,900 --> 00:09:25,620
The main one.

211
00:09:26,400 --> 00:09:29,380
And warm connection does not start with a deep conversation.

212
00:09:29,920 --> 00:09:31,090
It starts with hello.

213
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:35,540
Every good relationship in my life started with a first hello, Ben.

214
00:09:36,260 --> 00:09:37,460
Somebody said it first.

215
00:09:37,960 --> 00:09:38,760
My best friend.

216
00:09:39,240 --> 00:09:40,060
My sister-in-law.

217
00:09:40,800 --> 00:09:44,300
Somebody, one day, said the first small word.

218
00:09:45,660 --> 00:09:46,820
And your friends, Ben?

219
00:09:47,580 --> 00:09:49,040
Did they start with hello too?

220
00:09:50,400 --> 00:09:52,340
One of them started on a train, Mia.

221
00:09:53,060 --> 00:09:58,320
Eight years ago, a man asked me, "Is this seat free?" I said, "Yes.

222
00:09:58,980 --> 00:10:01,860
Hello." Just hello, after yes.

223
00:10:02,680 --> 00:10:06,000
And we talked for two hours, like old friends.

224
00:10:06,120 --> 00:10:06,830
About nothing.

225
00:10:07,540 --> 00:10:08,010
Football.

226
00:10:08,700 --> 00:10:09,080
Bread.

227
00:10:09,760 --> 00:10:10,350
His daughter.

228
00:10:11,260 --> 00:10:13,100
And at the end, he gave me his number.

229
00:10:13,740 --> 00:10:15,000
Did you call him, Ben?

230
00:10:15,740 --> 00:10:17,020
I almost did not, Mia.

231
00:10:17,480 --> 00:10:18,380
It felt strange.

232
00:10:19,180 --> 00:10:19,960
But I called.

233
00:10:20,620 --> 00:10:22,360
And that man was at my wedding.

234
00:10:23,060 --> 00:10:25,210
He is one of my three real friends.

235
00:10:25,760 --> 00:10:29,300
From "Is this seat free?" and "Yes.

236
00:10:30,100 --> 00:10:30,340
Hello."

237
00:10:31,100 --> 00:10:33,840
From "Is this seat free" to a wedding, Ben.

238
00:10:34,450 --> 00:10:36,460
That is the whole study in one train.

239
00:10:37,179 --> 00:10:40,380
And I think we make the word "friend" too big.

240
00:10:40,440 --> 00:10:43,260
We think a friend is a person we have known for ten years.

241
00:10:43,840 --> 00:10:46,820
But every ten-year friend was a stranger first.

242
00:10:47,320 --> 00:10:48,240
A hello first.

243
00:10:48,760 --> 00:10:49,760
A coffee second.

244
00:10:50,580 --> 00:10:52,960
A friend is a hello that continued.

245
00:10:53,560 --> 00:10:58,130
"A friend is a hello that continued." Mia, anyone can start one.

246
00:10:58,740 --> 00:10:59,120
Today.

247
00:11:00,200 --> 00:11:02,060
Can I ask about your loneliest year?

248
00:11:02,860 --> 00:11:05,520
You told me once, and I think the study explains it.

249
00:11:06,460 --> 00:11:09,670
When I moved to this city, Ben, I knew nobody.

250
00:11:09,700 --> 00:11:12,690
For six months, I talked to nobody except at work.

251
00:11:13,290 --> 00:11:14,780
And I told myself it was fine.

252
00:11:15,220 --> 00:11:16,180
I was busy.

253
00:11:16,320 --> 00:11:18,060
I was independent.

254
00:11:18,070 --> 00:11:19,960
But I was not fine.

255
00:11:20,020 --> 00:11:22,080
I was tired all the time.

256
00:11:22,120 --> 00:11:23,320
I got sick twice.

257
00:11:23,860 --> 00:11:24,860
I did not sleep.

258
00:11:25,480 --> 00:11:27,040
Loneliness and the body, Mia.

259
00:11:27,470 --> 00:11:28,580
The study connects them.

260
00:11:29,420 --> 00:11:31,360
And you did not connect them, at the time?

261
00:11:32,060 --> 00:11:32,900
Not at all, Ben.

262
00:11:33,440 --> 00:11:34,660
I thought I was just tired.

263
00:11:35,300 --> 00:11:38,120
And then, after six months, a hello.

264
00:11:38,800 --> 00:11:39,560
Of course.

265
00:11:40,200 --> 00:11:45,920
A woman at the gym said, "Hello, you are here every Tuesday, aren't you?" And that was it.

266
00:11:46,660 --> 00:11:47,720
She said it first.

267
00:11:48,160 --> 00:11:50,620
And we had coffee, and we talked for an hour.

268
00:11:51,100 --> 00:11:52,280
And she had friends.

269
00:11:52,810 --> 00:11:55,310
And by the end of the year, I had people.

270
00:11:56,100 --> 00:11:59,240
From one hello that you did not even say, Mia.

271
00:11:59,420 --> 00:12:00,140
She said it.

272
00:12:00,820 --> 00:12:01,930
Somebody went first.

273
00:12:02,530 --> 00:12:04,840
And I think she did not know what she was doing.

274
00:12:05,480 --> 00:12:06,940
She thought she was being polite.

275
00:12:07,620 --> 00:12:09,120
She was saving your year.

276
00:12:09,840 --> 00:12:11,520
"She thought she was being polite.

277
00:12:12,100 --> 00:12:16,620
She was saving my year." Ben, that is the whole episode, I think.

278
00:12:17,440 --> 00:12:20,820
We never know what our hello is doing on the other side.

279
00:12:20,900 --> 00:12:22,100
It looks like nothing.

280
00:12:22,860 --> 00:12:24,240
It might be everything.

281
00:12:25,520 --> 00:12:27,200
Okay, so that was part two.

282
00:12:27,800 --> 00:12:29,120
Why hello matters.

283
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:32,530
We said good relationships are the main thing.

284
00:12:33,069 --> 00:12:36,760
And we said a friend is a hello that continued.

285
00:12:36,840 --> 00:12:37,849
Now, part three.

286
00:12:38,700 --> 00:12:40,690
Simple ways to say hello first.

287
00:12:41,460 --> 00:12:42,640
Ben, you go first.

288
00:12:43,800 --> 00:12:44,540
Of course, Mia.

289
00:12:45,580 --> 00:12:46,649
Okay, part three.

290
00:12:47,460 --> 00:12:49,460
My first idea is counting.

291
00:12:49,540 --> 00:12:53,180
Last month, I said hello to everyone I passed at work for one week.

292
00:12:53,220 --> 00:12:53,980
The cleaner.

293
00:12:54,040 --> 00:12:54,900
The security man.

294
00:12:55,220 --> 00:12:56,160
The people in the lift.

295
00:12:56,640 --> 00:12:57,960
Not a long talk.

296
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:00,260
Hello, and their name if I knew it.

297
00:13:00,320 --> 00:13:02,680
And by Friday, the building felt different.

298
00:13:02,720 --> 00:13:03,100
Warmer.

299
00:13:03,640 --> 00:13:05,510
People smiled at me before I smiled.

300
00:13:06,040 --> 00:13:07,120
Some of them are friends now.

301
00:13:07,960 --> 00:13:10,280
Ten seconds a day changed the building, Ben.

302
00:13:11,120 --> 00:13:14,620
My first idea is for shy people, because I am one.

303
00:13:15,440 --> 00:13:18,000
Saying hello first is harder for us.

304
00:13:18,020 --> 00:13:19,040
It is a real fear.

305
00:13:19,820 --> 00:13:22,000
So here is a very small hello.

306
00:13:22,660 --> 00:13:25,060
A nod, and a smile, and the word.

307
00:13:25,990 --> 00:13:28,000
"Hello." One word.

308
00:13:28,360 --> 00:13:29,180
Not a question.

309
00:13:29,700 --> 00:13:31,460
Just the word, with a smile.

310
00:13:32,080 --> 00:13:33,500
You do not need to talk.

311
00:13:34,280 --> 00:13:35,620
And then keep walking.

312
00:13:36,320 --> 00:13:37,840
And nobody needs to answer, Mia.

313
00:13:38,420 --> 00:13:40,100
It is a gift, not a request.

314
00:13:40,740 --> 00:13:41,780
And most people smile.

315
00:13:42,380 --> 00:13:43,360
Shy hellos count.

316
00:13:43,780 --> 00:13:44,560
They count fully.

317
00:13:45,460 --> 00:13:47,400
My second idea is about the other side.

318
00:13:48,040 --> 00:13:50,220
Sometimes we say hello, and it does not work.

319
00:13:50,820 --> 00:13:52,020
The person does not answer.

320
00:13:52,600 --> 00:13:53,340
Or they look away.

321
00:13:53,890 --> 00:13:55,360
And the fear says, "See?

322
00:13:55,720 --> 00:13:56,680
You should not have tried."

323
00:13:57,620 --> 00:13:59,240
And what do you say to that fear, Ben?

324
00:13:59,820 --> 00:14:01,080
Because it is loud.

325
00:14:01,440 --> 00:14:02,780
It says, "See?

326
00:14:03,260 --> 00:14:04,440
Do not try again."

327
00:14:05,400 --> 00:14:07,500
It happens, maybe one time in ten, Mia.

328
00:14:08,180 --> 00:14:09,780
And it is almost never about you.

329
00:14:10,380 --> 00:14:11,420
The person is tired.

330
00:14:11,880 --> 00:14:12,420
Or shy.

331
00:14:13,080 --> 00:14:14,740
Or having the worst day of their year.

332
00:14:15,500 --> 00:14:17,060
Their silence is their silence.

333
00:14:17,660 --> 00:14:18,920
Your hello was still good.

334
00:14:19,830 --> 00:14:22,780
"Their silence is their silence." Yes, Ben.

335
00:14:23,460 --> 00:14:26,960
And the one time in ten is not a reason to stop the nine.

336
00:14:27,600 --> 00:14:29,620
So I keep a small count.

337
00:14:29,680 --> 00:14:30,220
In my head.

338
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:33,740
This week, I said hello first eight times.

339
00:14:34,280 --> 00:14:37,000
Seven smiles, and maybe one new friend.

340
00:14:37,540 --> 00:14:38,520
One nothing.

341
00:14:39,320 --> 00:14:40,840
Seven is the number.

342
00:14:40,880 --> 00:14:42,140
Not one.

343
00:14:42,300 --> 00:14:43,260
I count the seven.

344
00:14:44,320 --> 00:14:47,160
"I count the seven." Mia, that is a good rule.

345
00:14:48,060 --> 00:14:49,590
My third idea is about home.

346
00:14:50,280 --> 00:14:52,780
Because there is a hello I always forget.

347
00:14:52,819 --> 00:14:56,660
I say hello to the bread man, and then I come home, and I say nothing to my wife.

348
00:14:57,120 --> 00:14:57,900
My best friend.

349
00:14:58,600 --> 00:15:01,320
I walk in, put the bag down, look at the phone.

350
00:15:01,800 --> 00:15:02,340
No hello.

351
00:15:03,320 --> 00:15:06,880
And she is the most important person in your whole study, Ben.

352
00:15:07,600 --> 00:15:11,079
The one warm connection the Harvard study is really about.

353
00:15:11,820 --> 00:15:14,340
And I give her the smallest hello of the day, Mia.

354
00:15:15,020 --> 00:15:15,500
Or none.

355
00:15:16,420 --> 00:15:17,860
So now, I stop at the door.

356
00:15:18,560 --> 00:15:19,740
I put the bag down.

357
00:15:19,780 --> 00:15:23,720
And I say hello first, like she is a person I want to talk to.

358
00:15:23,780 --> 00:15:24,520
Because she is.

359
00:15:25,410 --> 00:15:32,960
"Like she is a person I want to talk to." Ben, that is beautiful, and a little sad, because we all do this.

360
00:15:33,620 --> 00:15:39,020
The hello goes to strangers, and the people at home get the tired face, and no talk.

361
00:15:39,880 --> 00:15:44,139
My mother calls me, and I say, "Yes, what?" Not hello.

362
00:15:44,850 --> 00:15:46,790
"Yes, what?" To my mother.

363
00:15:47,609 --> 00:15:49,960
"Yes, what?" To your mother, Mia.

364
00:15:50,760 --> 00:15:52,300
I think my mother gets the same from me.

365
00:15:53,140 --> 00:15:54,260
So what will you do this week?

366
00:15:55,140 --> 00:15:58,400
This week, I am starting my calls with hello, Ben.

367
00:15:59,200 --> 00:16:00,120
A real one.

368
00:16:00,750 --> 00:16:01,500
"Hello, Mom.

369
00:16:01,740 --> 00:16:04,620
How are you?" And waiting for the answer.

370
00:16:05,500 --> 00:16:07,020
Waiting is part of hello.

371
00:16:07,740 --> 00:16:10,240
A hello without waiting is just a noise.

372
00:16:11,580 --> 00:16:13,070
Do you have a fourth idea?

373
00:16:13,680 --> 00:16:16,300
Something for the evening, maybe?

374
00:16:16,340 --> 00:16:16,920
Yes, Mia.

375
00:16:17,480 --> 00:16:20,780
For many people, the evening is the loneliest time.

376
00:16:20,900 --> 00:16:21,980
So, a message.

377
00:16:22,680 --> 00:16:24,310
One line, to one person.

378
00:16:25,330 --> 00:16:25,640
"Hello.

379
00:16:26,200 --> 00:16:28,570
I thought about you today." That is all.

380
00:16:29,340 --> 00:16:30,009
No question.

381
00:16:30,520 --> 00:16:31,040
No plan.

382
00:16:31,840 --> 00:16:33,160
I do this three times a week.

383
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:34,980
My cousin.

384
00:16:35,020 --> 00:16:35,680
An old friend.

385
00:16:36,280 --> 00:16:37,280
A colleague who left.

386
00:16:38,080 --> 00:16:41,060
And most of the time, they write back, and we talk a little.

387
00:16:42,360 --> 00:16:44,119
Three messages a week, Ben.

388
00:16:44,600 --> 00:16:47,400
That is nothing, and it is a lot.

389
00:16:47,960 --> 00:16:49,720
I want to try that.

390
00:16:49,780 --> 00:16:52,220
And for the people who are learning English?

391
00:16:52,260 --> 00:16:56,180
Because I think hello is the best English lesson nobody teaches.

392
00:16:57,120 --> 00:16:57,720
It is, Mia.

393
00:16:58,480 --> 00:17:01,020
Many learners are afraid to talk to people in English.

394
00:17:01,780 --> 00:17:03,790
They wait until they are ready.

395
00:17:03,840 --> 00:17:05,100
Until the grammar is good.

396
00:17:05,940 --> 00:17:07,720
But hello does not need grammar.

397
00:17:08,480 --> 00:17:09,860
Hello is one word.

398
00:17:10,910 --> 00:17:11,220
"Hello.

399
00:17:11,700 --> 00:17:13,560
Nice day." Two words.

400
00:17:14,380 --> 00:17:14,680
"Hello.

401
00:17:15,200 --> 00:17:17,090
Long day?" Three words.

402
00:17:17,700 --> 00:17:19,420
And that is a real conversation.

403
00:17:20,260 --> 00:17:22,120
You can make a friend with three words.

404
00:17:23,520 --> 00:17:26,380
My first Spanish conversation was two words, Ben.

405
00:17:27,080 --> 00:17:27,480
"Hola.

406
00:17:27,860 --> 00:17:29,840
Qué calor." Hello.

407
00:17:30,360 --> 00:17:31,540
So hot.

408
00:17:31,580 --> 00:17:33,280
To a man at a bus stop.

409
00:17:33,320 --> 00:17:35,960
And he said, "Mucho." A lot.

410
00:17:36,510 --> 00:17:37,240
And that was it.

411
00:17:37,880 --> 00:17:39,480
My first real Spanish.

412
00:17:40,200 --> 00:17:41,590
I went home so happy.

413
00:17:42,820 --> 00:17:44,500
Two words, and you went home happy.

414
00:17:45,340 --> 00:17:48,820
Mia, if you can say hello, you can speak English today.

415
00:17:49,660 --> 00:17:50,660
Not after the course.

416
00:17:51,240 --> 00:17:52,440
Today.

417
00:17:52,450 --> 00:17:54,620
You can make a friend in English today.

418
00:17:55,560 --> 00:17:59,580
And after hello comes talk, and some people are afraid of that part.

419
00:18:00,460 --> 00:18:02,860
"What if they want to talk, and I have nothing to say?"

420
00:18:04,100 --> 00:18:05,760
That is my fear exactly, Ben.

421
00:18:06,360 --> 00:18:07,280
What is the secret?

422
00:18:08,400 --> 00:18:10,080
You do not need something to say, Mia.

423
00:18:10,900 --> 00:18:12,820
You need one question.

424
00:18:12,850 --> 00:18:20,480
"Long day?" "Busy today?" "How is your morning?" One small question, and then you listen.

425
00:18:21,360 --> 00:18:23,860
People love to talk about their day.

426
00:18:23,920 --> 00:18:24,900
Nobody asks them.

427
00:18:25,760 --> 00:18:34,860
You ask, and they talk, and you say "really?" and "that sounds hard." That is a conversation, and now you have a friend.

428
00:18:35,820 --> 00:18:37,580
You said ten words.

429
00:18:37,640 --> 00:18:38,900
They said two hundred.

430
00:18:40,340 --> 00:18:41,880
"You said ten words.

431
00:18:41,960 --> 00:18:48,660
They said two hundred." Ben, that is the best English conversation advice I have ever heard.

432
00:18:48,720 --> 00:18:51,270
Because the listener does not need grammar.

433
00:18:51,300 --> 00:18:55,000
The listener needs "really?" and a face that cares.

434
00:18:56,220 --> 00:18:57,320
A face that cares.

435
00:18:58,120 --> 00:19:02,280
And one last idea, Mia, for the people who are new somewhere.

436
00:19:02,360 --> 00:19:05,130
A new city, a new job, a new country.

437
00:19:06,020 --> 00:19:09,480
When you are new, everyone else looks like they already have friends.

438
00:19:10,340 --> 00:19:11,600
They do not.

439
00:19:11,680 --> 00:19:14,040
Most people are waiting, like at the party.

440
00:19:14,860 --> 00:19:16,380
Like at the party, Ben.

441
00:19:17,020 --> 00:19:18,000
I saw this at work.

442
00:19:18,500 --> 00:19:21,780
A new colleague, from Brazil, English not perfect.

443
00:19:22,360 --> 00:19:25,540
She said hello to everyone, by name, in week one.

444
00:19:26,160 --> 00:19:30,880
By week three, she had more friends than I had after five years.

445
00:19:30,939 --> 00:19:32,440
First beats perfect.

446
00:19:33,830 --> 00:19:37,120
"First beats perfect." Mia, that is our message today.

447
00:19:37,800 --> 00:19:41,400
Good relationships are the main thing, for your health and your happiness.

448
00:19:42,080 --> 00:19:44,380
And every good relationship starts with a first hello.

449
00:19:45,300 --> 00:19:46,979
Every friend was a stranger first.

450
00:19:47,540 --> 00:19:48,550
Somebody says it first.

451
00:19:49,280 --> 00:19:50,720
People remember who said hello.

452
00:19:51,620 --> 00:19:52,840
Let it be you.

453
00:19:52,860 --> 00:19:53,180
Today.

454
00:19:53,910 --> 00:19:57,060
To the neighbor, the bread man, your mother, your wife.

455
00:19:57,860 --> 00:19:58,380
One word.

456
00:19:59,160 --> 00:20:00,680
It looks like nothing.

457
00:20:00,720 --> 00:20:01,760
It might be everything.

458
00:20:02,760 --> 00:20:04,200
It might be everything.

459
00:20:04,960 --> 00:20:10,200
Ben, let's repeat today's important words, slowly, because our listeners want to remember them.

460
00:20:11,420 --> 00:20:11,800
Hello.

461
00:20:12,400 --> 00:20:13,220
The first word.

462
00:20:13,860 --> 00:20:14,680
Say hello.

463
00:20:15,380 --> 00:20:16,700
A hello is a door.

464
00:20:18,020 --> 00:20:18,380
People.

465
00:20:19,020 --> 00:20:20,300
Persons, others.

466
00:20:20,800 --> 00:20:21,860
The people we pass.

467
00:20:22,540 --> 00:20:23,900
People love to talk.

468
00:20:25,240 --> 00:20:25,640
First.

469
00:20:26,180 --> 00:20:27,620
Before others.

470
00:20:27,700 --> 00:20:28,460
Say it first.

471
00:20:29,220 --> 00:20:30,580
Somebody goes first.

472
00:20:31,820 --> 00:20:32,190
Friend.

473
00:20:32,880 --> 00:20:34,540
A person you know and like.

474
00:20:35,120 --> 00:20:36,540
A friend from one hello.

475
00:20:37,940 --> 00:20:38,280
Talk.

476
00:20:38,840 --> 00:20:39,900
To speak with someone.

477
00:20:40,420 --> 00:20:41,640
Talk for five minutes.

478
00:20:42,540 --> 00:20:43,940
Ask, and let them talk.

479
00:20:45,520 --> 00:20:45,820
Hello.

480
00:20:46,420 --> 00:20:46,750
People.

481
00:20:47,740 --> 00:20:48,080
First.

482
00:20:49,140 --> 00:20:49,460
Friend.

483
00:20:50,560 --> 00:20:50,840
Talk.

484
00:20:52,060 --> 00:20:53,920
Five words, and one to say tomorrow.

485
00:20:54,520 --> 00:20:55,620
And one big idea.

486
00:20:56,420 --> 00:20:57,360
Say hello first.

487
00:20:58,280 --> 00:21:00,800
Now, Mia, do you have a question for our listeners?

488
00:21:01,940 --> 00:21:02,520
Yes, Ben.

489
00:21:02,980 --> 00:21:05,030
Everyone listening, here is my question.

490
00:21:05,880 --> 00:21:07,360
Who is your silent person?

491
00:21:07,880 --> 00:21:10,460
The one you pass every day and never talk to.

492
00:21:11,200 --> 00:21:13,860
Write it in the comments in one short sentence.

493
00:21:14,320 --> 00:21:15,100
Not their name.

494
00:21:15,480 --> 00:21:16,560
Just who.

495
00:21:17,270 --> 00:21:27,980
"The woman at the bakery." "My neighbor with the dog." And then, if you want, add, "Tomorrow I say hello first." And Ben will tell us the bread man's name.

496
00:21:29,440 --> 00:21:29,920
I will, Mia.

497
00:21:30,440 --> 00:21:31,850
Next episode, first thing.

498
00:21:32,540 --> 00:21:36,440
And if this episode helped you, please subscribe to Two Cups English.

499
00:21:36,480 --> 00:21:38,900
We make a new episode every few days.

500
00:21:39,060 --> 00:21:43,760
Slow English, simple words, and one true story each time.

501
00:21:43,820 --> 00:21:46,700
The full text of this episode is in the description.

502
00:21:46,760 --> 00:21:49,489
Read it, and then go say hello to someone.

503
00:21:49,580 --> 00:21:50,480
That is the homework.

504
00:21:51,300 --> 00:21:51,820
One word.

505
00:21:53,020 --> 00:21:54,880
Thank you for spending this time with us.

506
00:21:55,280 --> 00:21:57,800
Maybe you are on a bus right now, next to someone.

507
00:21:58,500 --> 00:21:59,780
Maybe you are in a hallway.

508
00:22:00,460 --> 00:22:01,840
Maybe your mother is calling.

509
00:22:02,620 --> 00:22:05,320
Say hello first, to a stranger or a friend.

510
00:22:05,820 --> 00:22:07,320
A real one, with waiting.

511
00:22:07,960 --> 00:22:09,019
It looks like nothing.

512
00:22:09,560 --> 00:22:10,920
It might be everything.

513
00:22:11,960 --> 00:22:12,580
This is Ben.

514
00:22:13,340 --> 00:22:14,300
And this is Mia.

515
00:22:15,120 --> 00:22:16,480
This was Two Cups English.

516
00:22:17,180 --> 00:22:18,160
Say hello first.

517
00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:19,900
We will see you next time.

